problem:
Problem description
- There is a single-period transportation planning problem with a set of supply locations $$\mathcal{I}$$ indexed by $$i$$ and a set of destination ports $$\mathcal{J}$$ indexed by $$j$$.
- Each warehouse $$i \in \mathcal{I}$$ has an available inventory of empty containers denoted by $$s_i$$.
- Each port $$j \in \mathcal{J}$$ has a required container demand denoted by $$d_j$$ that must be fulfilled.
- The transportation distance between warehouse $$i$$ and port $$j$$ is denoted by $$\ell_{ij}$$.
- Transportation cost is linear in shipped containers and distance, with a common cost rate denoted by $$\rho$$ per kilometer per container.
- Transportation is carried out by whole trucks, each with a maximum carrying capacity denoted by $$u$$ containers.
- The planning goal is to choose a minimum-cost shipping policy that allocates containers from warehouses to ports and determines the needed truck usage on each lane.
- Business constraints are:
  - Only containers available in the warehouse network may be shipped.
  - Every port demand must be satisfied.
  - Shipments are assigned to exactly one destination port per container.
  - Truck usage on any lane must be sufficient to carry the containers assigned to that lane, respecting the truck capacity $$u$$.
  - Container quantities and truck counts are integral.
  - Any excess inventory may remain unused at warehouses if total supply exceeds total demand.
- A solution is represented by:
  - $$x_{ij}$$: the number of containers shipped from warehouse $$i$$ to port $$j$$.
  - $$y_{ij}$$: the number of trucks deployed on the route from warehouse $$i$$ to port $$j$$.
  - $$r_i$$: the number of containers left unused at warehouse $$i$$ after the transportation plan.

Input Format
{
  "warehouses": "array of labels for the set $$\\mathcal{I}$$",
  "ports": "array of labels for the set $$\\mathcal{J}$$",
  "supply": {
    "shape": "$$|\\mathcal{I}|$$",
    "meaning": "available inventory vector $$s$$, where $$s_i$$ is the number of containers at warehouse $$i$$"
  },
  "demand": {
    "shape": "$$|\\mathcal{J}|$$",
    "meaning": "required demand vector $$d$$, where $$d_j$$ is the number of containers required at port $$j$$"
  },
  "distance": {
    "shape": "$$|\\mathcal{I}| \\times |\\mathcal{J}|$$",
    "meaning": "distance matrix $$\\ell$$, where $$\\ell_{ij}$$ is the distance from warehouse $$i$$ to port $$j$$"
  },
  "cost_rate": {
    "shape": "$$1$$",
    "meaning": "transport cost rate $$\\rho$$ per kilometer per container"
  },
  "truck_capacity": {
    "shape": "$$1$$",
    "meaning": "truck capacity $$u$$ measured in containers per truck"
  }
}

Output Format
{
  "shipments": {
    "shape": "$$|\\mathcal{I}| \\times |\\mathcal{J}|$$",
    "meaning": "shipment matrix $$x$$, where $$x_{ij}$$ is the number of containers sent from warehouse $$i$$ to port $$j$$"
  },
  "trucks": {
    "shape": "$$|\\mathcal{I}| \\times |\\mathcal{J}|$$",
    "meaning": "truck matrix $$y$$, where $$y_{ij}$$ is the number of trucks used on route $$i \\to j$$"
  },
  "unused_inventory": {
    "shape": "$$|\\mathcal{I}|$$",
    "meaning": "remaining inventory vector $$r$$, where $$r_i$$ is the number of containers left at warehouse $$i$$"
  },
  "objective_value": {
    "shape": "$$1$$",
    "meaning": "minimum total transportation cost achieved by the plan"
  }
}

Example(s)

Input 1
{
  "warehouses": ["Verona", "Perugia", "Rome", "Pescara", "Taranto", "Lamezia"],
  "ports": ["Genoa", "Venice", "Ancona", "Naples", "Bari"],
  "supply": [10, 12, 20, 24, 18, 40],
  "demand": [20, 15, 25, 33, 21],
  "distance": [
    [290, 115, 355, 715, 810],
    [380, 340, 165, 380, 610],
    [505, 530, 285, 220, 450],
    [655, 450, 155, 240, 315],
    [1010, 840, 550, 305, 95],
    [1072, 1097, 747, 372, 333]
  ],
  "cost_rate": 30,
  "truck_capacity": 2
}
